  "context_pill": "Scheduled Bank: Bad Debts vs Provision",
  "example_summary": "This example calculates the net deduction for bad debts for a Scheduled Bank by applying the proviso to Section 36(1)(vii), which requires setting off actual bad debts against the total provision balance created under Section 36(1)(viia).",
  "source_provision": "### Statutory Interplay: Section 36(1)(vii) & Section 36(1)(viia)\n\n| Statutory Provision | Target Assessee | Quantitative Limit / Allowance | Statutory Basis |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Section 36(1)(viia)** | Scheduled Bank / Indian Bank | 8.5% of Gross Total Income + 10% of Aggregate Average Rural Advances | Provision for Bad & Doubtful Debts |\n| **Section 36(1)(vii)** | All Assessees (including Banks) | Actual Bad Debts written off in books of account | Direct Write-off Deduction |\n| **Proviso to Sec 36(1)(vii)** | Assessees covered u/s 36(1)(viia) | Restricted to: Write-off minus Closing Credit Balance of Provision | Proviso Excess Test |\n\n\n#### Key Statutory Conditions Checklist\n\n* **Current Year Provision Ceiling u/s 36(1)(viia):** Computed as 8.5% of Gross Total Income (before deduction u/s 36(1)(viia) and Chapter VI-A) **PLUS** 10% of Aggregate Average Advances (AAA) made by rural branches.\n* **Cumulative Credit Balance Rule:** Credit balance of provision u/s 36(1)(viia) equals **Opening Provision brought forward** PLUS **Current Year Provision allowed** u/s 36(1)(viia).\n* **Net Allowable Write-off u/s 36(1)(vii):** $\\text{Allowable Bad Debts Deduction} = \\text{Bad Debts written off} - \\text{Total Provision Balance u/s 36(1)(viia)}$.\n\n\n> [!IMPORTANT] Interplay Trap: Double Deduction Prohibition\n> A bank cannot claim full deduction for bad debts written off without reducing the existing provision credit balance. The proviso ensures that debts are first set off against provisions created u/s 36(1)(viia), avoiding double tax deduction.",
  "director": {
